<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bentivoglio family</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bentivoglio were a family who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> as unofficial lords for much of the 15th century, under the nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the Papal States. After Giovanni I briefly seized power in 1401, Annibale, Sante and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-ii-bentivoglio">Giovanni II Bentivoglio</a> dominated the city's government from the 1440s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> drove Giovanni II out in 1506, ending their rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bentivoglio-family.jpg" alt="Bentivoglio family" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MostEpic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_house_of_Bentivoglio.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family's presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> is first recorded in 1323. It took its name from the castle of Bentivoglio near the city and claimed descent from Enzio, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, an illegitimate son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Holy Roman Emperor Frederick II</a>. Belonging to one of Bologna's workers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, the Bentivoglio gained power in the 14th century as pro-papal Guelph leaders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Amid the faction conflicts of the commune, Giovanni I declared himself signore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonfaloniere-of-justice">Gonfaloniere di Giustizia</a> on 14 March 1401 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a>. The Visconti turned against him, and he was killed at the Battle of Casalecchio on 26 June 1402. His son Anton Galeazzo, a lecturer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, held power briefly in 1420 before being overthrown, became a condottiero and was assassinated by papal officials in 1435.</p><p>In 1438 Annibale I, a putative son of Anton Galeazzo, led a revolt against papal rule. Imprisoned by the Visconti condottiere <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-piccinino">Niccolò Piccinino</a> in 1442 and freed by Galeazzo Marescotti, he returned to be given the powers of government, but was assassinated in 1445. His successor Sante I, of doubtful parentage and once an apprentice in the wool <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, was advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> and brought a short period of calm, with closer ties to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Florence. Annibale's son Giovanni II then ruled as virtual tyrant until his expulsion.</p><p>Giovanni II's son Annibale II, who married Lucrezia d'Este, daughter of Duke Ercole I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> with French help during a rebellion against Julius II in 1511, but ruled only a year before he was assassinated. He was the last ruler of his line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In exile the family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, where it produced prelates such as Cardinal Guido Bentivoglio, a papal diplomat and historian of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> who was among the inquisitors who signed the condemnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> the Church of San Giacomo Maggiore was the centre of their power; it holds the tomb of Anton Galeazzo by Jacopo della Quercia and the family chapel, with paintings by Lorenzo Costa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bentivoglio family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nominally under the Papal States, the city was dominated by the Pepoli and then the <b>Bentivoglio</b> families.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Republic of Bologna</a></li></ul></section>
